      "source_example_id": "e-14659",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Number",
      "kp_id": "e-14659"
    },
    "q-213383": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213383",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $32,$ the additive inverse of $19$ modulo $32$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $19$ modulo $32$ is the integer $0\\le x<32$ such that\n\n$$\nx+19≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,32).\n$$\n\nNotice that $-19+19≡0,$ so $x≡-19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,32).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-19 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,32) \\\\  & ≡0-19 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,32) \\\\  & ≡32-19 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,32) \\\\  & ≡13 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,32)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $19$ modulo $32$ is $13.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n13+19≡32≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,32)\n$$\n\n**Note:** The additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
