    "q-213352": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213352",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $12,$ the additive inverse of $-18$ modulo $12$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $-18$ modulo $12$ is the integer $0\\le x<12$ such that\n\n$$\nx+(-18)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $-18≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-18 & ≡(-2)\\cdot 12+6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡(-2)\\cdot 0+6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-6+6≡0,$ so $x≡-6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡0-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡12-6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡6 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-18$ modulo $12$ is $6.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-18+6≡-12≡(-1)\\cdot 12≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$",
